1
00:05:13,118 --> 00:05:15,798
The International Space
Station is falling right

2
00:05:15,798 --> 00:05:16,598
now.

3
00:05:16,758 --> 00:05:19,058
It has been for
more than 25 years.

4
00:05:19,958 --> 00:05:21,258
So why doesn't it
hit the ground?

5
00:05:22,098 --> 00:05:25,438
Up there, about 400
kilometres above us, gravity

6
00:05:25,438 --> 00:05:28,718
is still about 90% as
strong as it is down here.

7
00:05:29,238 --> 00:05:31,518
If the station just stopped,
it would come straight

8
00:05:31,518 --> 00:05:32,318
down.

9
00:05:32,498 --> 00:05:34,578
The trick is that it's
also moving sideways.

10
00:05:35,338 --> 00:05:36,138
Very fast.

11
00:05:37,038 --> 00:05:39,378
About 8 kilometres every second.

12
00:05:39,378 --> 00:05:41,178
Picture throwing a ball.

13
00:05:41,678 --> 00:05:43,538
It curves down and lands.

14
00:05:44,498 --> 00:05:46,898
Throw it harder, and
it lands further away.

15
00:05:47,678 --> 00:05:50,238
Throw it hard enough, and
the ground curves away

16
00:05:50,238 --> 00:05:52,438
beneath it as fast as it falls.

17
00:05:53,318 --> 00:05:55,398
It keeps falling,
and it keeps missing.

18
00:05:56,138 --> 00:05:56,938
That's an orbit.

19
00:05:57,378 --> 00:05:59,558
The station goes all the
way around the Earth about

20
00:05:59,558 --> 00:06:02,238
every 90 minutes,
16 times a day.

21
00:06:03,038 --> 00:06:05,558
And the astronauts float,
because they're falling

22
00:06:05,558 --> 00:06:09,618
too, at exactly the same
rate as the station around

23
00:06:09,618 --> 00:06:10,418
them.

24
00:06:10,678 --> 00:06:13,338
Even so, at that height,
there's still a tiny bit

25
00:06:13,338 --> 00:06:16,418
of air, and it slowly
drags the station lower.

26
00:06:17,098 --> 00:06:19,778
So every now and then,
an engine gives it a push

27
00:06:19,778 --> 00:06:20,938
to climb back up.

28
00:06:21,398 --> 00:06:25,338
Around 2030, NASA plans
to stop pushing and steer

29
00:06:25,338 --> 00:06:27,318
it down into a remote
part of the ocean.
